# AGENTS.md — <project>

Entry file for any coding agent in this lab repo (`CLAUDE.md` may be a
symlink to it). The rules behind every step are the lab standard that ships
with the pinned labkit: `python -m labkit.rules show LAB-nnn` prints one;
a gate message names the rule it enforces by id and name.

## Lane `seconds` — one number in minutes

Applies when the protocol head says `cost = "seconds"`: a run that can be
repeated at will (a count, a handful of model calls for a minute and a cent),
LAB-017 The number of audits follows the cost tier.

1. `labkit-init exp <name> --cost seconds` (`--model <alias>` when a model is
   in the loop) — stamps the seconds cut of the protocol and the matching
   skeleton.
2. Write the registration: `protocol-fill <PROTOCOL> --list` names every
   open field, the TOML head's `type` first (`cost = "seconds"` is stamped).
   A count owes question · data · exclusions · unit; with a model also the
   success criterion, the admissibility condition, the prompt and the null
   condition. Every coinage of your own — a constant such as a margin, arms,
   item ids, the model alias — gets half a sentence under "Terms of this
   protocol" — a count with no coinage writes "n/a — no coinage". Fill by
   label, all in one call: `protocol-fill <PROTOCOL> "type=exploratory"
   "<Label>=<value>" ...` (one quoted word per field); the prompt block from
   the script's `PROMPT` constants with `protocol-fill <PROTOCOL> --prompts`.
3. Fill the skeleton (with a model: read `--show 2`), run `code-check <area>`.
4. Commit, then the run — in the foreground; it is the instrument check too.
5. `protocol-fill PROTOCOL-<name>.md --from-run <run_id>` writes the number
   back from metrics.json: the Answer line, the Summary's Result line and the
   run line — never retype it (reword the Result line for a person if you
   like; Limits and Next step belong to the full form). Commit `output/` and
   `runs/` with the protocol — `finalize <area>` checks the provenance and
   wants both in git.
6. The agent audit is a later, separate task: due when the number goes into
   a note or a paper or steers a decision.

Nothing more is owed on this lane: no freeze, no probe before the run, no †
fields, no reading of labkit's source.

> **End of lane `seconds`.** For a number at `cost = "seconds"` stop here;
> read the area's `README.md` and nothing else of this file.

## Lane `minutes` / `hours`

1. `labkit-init exp <name>` — never `mkdir`. Stamps the protocol and the
   script skeletons (`scripts/00_prepare.py · 01_measure.py · 02_analyze.py`).
2. Read `protocol-check --contract`, then fill the TOML head (`type`, `cost`)
   and the Intent before the first call: hypothesis · success criterion ·
   admissibility condition, the prompt verbatim; an id `[C]/[A]/[B]` on every
   promise to the code (LAB-003 No run without an intent paragraph,
   LAB-004 Identifiers make the intent addressable).
3. `01_measure.py --show 3` — render check: read prompts on real data, no
   call (LAB-007 Render check before the instrument check).
4. After every code change `code-check <area>`
   (LAB-018 Routine check after every implementation).
5. `--probe` — instrument check in the real pipeline; it may change the
   instrument, never the hypothesis or the criteria; its numbers are never
   reported (LAB-008 Instrument check before freezing).
6. Resolution check as a test: fill `tests/test_<NN>_<name>.py`
   (LAB-016 Resolution check before freezing).
7. `preflight <area>`, then the preflight audit (`labkit-audit <area> --mode
   preflight`); decide the findings, the line with `scope sha` into the head
   (LAB-015 Preflight audit before freezing). A fix changes the stamp: audit
   the fixed code again before freezing — the head's `scope sha` must be one
   an audit record saw. An audit takes minutes, longer
   than many harnesses let a foreground command run: start it with your
   harness's background mechanism and wait for that task to end — not a
   `pgrep` loop, which finds its own command line and never ends.
8. `labkit-init exp <name> --report`, fill the † fields, only now
   `frozen = true` (LAB-011 Required-field check).
9. Commit first, then pilot → run, always through `runlog`; resume = the same
   command; state: `.venv/bin/runlog <area>` (LAB-010 Commit before the run,
   LAB-043 Runs go through `runlog`).
10. `finalize <area>`, then the analysis audit (`labkit-audit <area> --mode
    analysis`) against the frozen criterion
    (LAB-050 Analysis audit before reporting).
11. Write the result back into the protocol (`protocol-fill --from-run`),
    then `scripts/02_analyze.py --check` holds the note to the run, and
    `report-check <area>` closes the lane: frozen, finalize clean, an
    analysis audit of the code as it is, the check passing
    (LAB-041 Generate numbers, don't retype them).

## Setup

- One interpreter: `.venv/bin/python`; versions in `requirements.lock`,
  refrozen after every install. The editor uses the same
  (`.vscode/settings.json`).
- `python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt &&
  .venv/bin/labkit-hooks install` — the hooks come with the pinned labkit.
- Tests: `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q`.
- If your harness kills a foreground shell command after N seconds, set
  `LABKIT_FOREGROUND_TIMEOUT_S=N` in its configuration, so every command it
  spawns has it (Codex: `[shell_environment_policy] set = {
  LABKIT_FOREGROUND_TIMEOUT_S = "N" }` in `.codex/config.toml`; Claude Code
  is recognised without it). A long run then refuses the foreground and
  asks for `runlog start <area> -- <cmd>` instead of dying mid-run.

## Layout

- `experiments/NN-<name>/` — one area per question, numbered by creation.
- `data/raw/` → `data/prepared/` — materialized steps, read-only once written.
- `lib/` — repo-local helpers; shared helpers come as the pinned labkit
  package (`from labkit import runlog`), never as copies.
- `notes/` — `theorie/` (what holds independent of our runs), `messung/`
  (how we measure: instruments, protocols), `results/` (what a run showed).
- `models.toml` — the one place for model backends; code names an alias.
